@tommy
#regex-builders
:default

/rxe { Uses method chaining with readable names like .digit(), .one_or_more(), .literal(). Great for building complex patterns piece by piece. Patterns can be saved and reused as variables.

```python
email = (rxe
  .exactly(username)
  .literal('@')
  .exactly(domain)
)
```

Install: pip install git+git://github.com/mtrencseni/rxe }

/python-verbalexpressions { Python port of the popular VerbalExpressions family. Very readable fluent API with methods like .start_of_line(), .find(), .maybe(). Best for simpler patterns where extreme readability matters. Wide language support across many platforms.

```python
url = (VerEx()
  .find('http')
  .maybe('s')
  .find('://')
)
``` }

/regex-builder { Functional approach with immutable builder objects. Good for systematic construction with functions like literal(), range(), repeats(). Allows pattern reuse and composition. Less actively maintained than the others.

```python
number = optional(range('1-2')).range('0-9')
``` }

/regal { Uses Clojure data structures (vectors and keywords) to represent regex patterns, similar to Hiccup for HTML. Supports multiple regex flavors (Java, JavaScript) and can generate test data from patterns.

```clojure
(def r
  [:cat [:+ [:class [\\a \\z]]] "=" [:+ [:not \\=]]])
(regal/regex r) ;;=> #"[a-z]+\\Q=\\E[^=]+"
``` }

/regal 2:1 /regex-builder {more readable; the structure of the data DSL matches regex structure better, and you can use all of Clojure to build/combine patterns}
/regex-builder 21:20 /rxe {nearly indistinguishable; regex-builder feels more like expressions/combinators than a builder pattern}
/rxe 1:1 /python-verbalexpressions {the same}
